 We want to find the orthogonal projection of $9 e_1$ onto the subspace spanned by $\begin{bmatrix} 2 \\ 2 \\ 1 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix}$ and $\begin{bmatrix} -2 \\ 2 \\ 0 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}$. First, we need to find the orthogonal projection of $9e_1$ onto the span of the two vectors. Let's call this vector $\mathbf{v}$. Then we need to solve the system of equations \begin{align*}
a\begin{bmatrix} 2 \\ 2 \\ 1 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix}+b\begin{bmatrix} -2 \\ 2 \\ 0 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix} & = 9e_1 = \begin{bmatrix} 9 \\ 0 \\ 0 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix} \\
\Rightarrow \begin{bmatrix} 2a-2b \\ 2a+2b \\ a \\ b \end{bmatrix} & = \begin{bmatrix} 9 \\ 0 \\ 0 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix}
\end{align*}Solving this system yields $a = 5$ and $b = -2$. Therefore, \begin{align*}
\mathbf{v} &= 5\begin{bmatrix} 2 \\ 2 \\ 1 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix} - 2\begin{bmatrix} -2 \\ 2 \\ 0 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix} \\
&= \begin{bmatrix} 10 \\ 6 \\ 5 \\ -2 \end{bmatrix}
\end{align*}Since $\mathbf{v}$ is orthogonal to $\begin{bmatrix} 2 \\ 2 \\ 1 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix}$ and $\begin{bmatrix} -2 \\ 2 \\ 0 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}$, we can use the formula for the orthogonal projection to find that \begin{align*}
\mathrm{proj}_{\mathrm{span}\left(\begin{bmatrix} 2 \\ 2 \\ 1 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix}, \begin{bmatrix} -2 \\ 2 \\ 0 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}\right)\begin{bmatrix} 9 \\ 0 \\ 0 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix} &= \frac{\mathbf{v}\cdot \begin{bmatrix} 9 \\ 0 \\ 0 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix}}{\mathbf{v}\cdot \mathbf{v}} \mathbf{v} \\
&= \frac{\begin{bmatrix} 10 \\ 6 \\ 5 \\ -2 \end{bmatrix}\cdot \begin{bmatrix} 9 \\ 0 \\ 0 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix}}{\begin{bmatrix} 10 \\ 6 \\ 5 \\ -2 \end{bmatrix}\cdot \begin{bmatrix} 10 \\ 6 \\ 5 \\ -2 \end{bmatrix}} \begin{bmatrix} 10 \\ 6 \\ 5 \\ -2 \end{bmatrix} \\
&= \frac{90}{225} \begin{bmatrix} 10 \\ 6 \\ 5 \\ -2 \end{bmatrix} \\
&= \frac{2}{5} \begin{bmatrix} 10 \\ 6 \\ 5 \\ -2 \end{bmatrix} \\
&= \begin{bmatrix} 4 \\ 2.4 \\ 2 \\ -0.4 \end{bmatrix}
\end{align*}The answer is (D).
